<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Jaquijahuana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1548">1548</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Xaquixaguana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Jaquijahuana was fought on 9 April 1548 on the plain of Jaquijahuana near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> between royal forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-la-gasca">Pedro de la Gasca</a> and the rebel encomenderos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a>. Most of Pizarro's men deserted to Gasca, who had offered pardons, before the fighting began, and Pizarro surrendered. He and his commander Francisco de Carvajal were executed the next day, ending the rebellion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-jaquijahuana.jpg" alt="Battle of Jaquijahuana" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trabajo propio. Reproducción de un grabado de autor desconocido. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Jaquijahuana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle closed the Revolt of the Encomenderos among the Spanish conquerors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. After the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> in 1538, the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> in 1541 and the execution of Almagro's son, El Mozo, in 1542, most of the able commanders of New Castile had been lost. King Charles then sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a> as viceroy of Peru to enforce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a>, which protected the native population; he was sworn in at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 17 May 1544.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> refused to give up the power once held by his brothers. On 18 September he deposed the viceroy and sent him as a prisoner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, and on 28 October his army of 1,200 men entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. Released in Panama, Núñez Vela returned, but on 18 January 1546 Pizarro defeated him at Añaquito, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, and the viceroy was beheaded on the field.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The king appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-la-gasca">Pedro de la Gasca</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Landing in 1547 with inferior forces, Gasca won supporters by promising amnesty to those who had committed treason and by announcing that he would not enforce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a>, whose demands for better conditions for native labourers had driven many encomenderos to Pizarro. Late in 1547 the armies came close to battle on the plains of Jaquijahuana, but Gasca avoided it and used the time to win over more of Pizarro's officers, among them Alonso de Alvarado.</p><p>By April 1548 a steady string of defections had left Pizarro badly outnumbered and his men demoralised. Those who had not changed sides were killed or captured, while Gasca's army reportedly suffered a single casualty; Lope de Aguirre, fighting for the royalists, was wounded in the leg by a harquebus shot. Pizarro and Francisco de Carvajal, called the Demon of the Andes, fell into Gasca's hands; Pizarro was beheaded and Carvajal hanged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The defeat and execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> ended the rebellion that had begun when he refused to accept the viceroy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a>. Gasca then set about consolidating royal control over the country, which remained a royal colony and viceroyalty until the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> in the early 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Jaquijahuana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cleric Pedro de la Gasca, sent with broad powers and a promise to soften the laws, won over most of the rebels and defeated Gonzalo Pizarro at <b>Jaquijahuana</b> in 1548, after which Pizarro was executed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
